editorial:
## Method and reasoning
Pick a random pivot in the active interval. Separate values into less, equal, and greater regions with three pointers. The equal region already occupies its final rank interval. Discard both irrelevant regions and continue in the one containing k. Equal-heavy cases can finish in one pass.

During partition, [lo,less) is smaller, [less,i) is equal, [i,greater) is unknown, and [greater,hi) is larger. Swapping a large value with the right edge shrinks the unknown interval but leaves i unclassified. Advancing i in that branch could skip an element.

## Walk through the sample
Choosing pivot 4 in the sample creates three smaller values, three equal values, and one larger value. Ranks 4 through 6 all lie in the equal band, so rank 5 needs no further partitioning.

## Complexity
A uniformly random pivot lands in the middle half of the ranks with probability at least one half. Such a partition leaves at most about three quarters of the active interval; repeated constant-factor reductions give expected O(n) time. Repeated extreme pivots can take O(n²). The partition uses O(1) auxiliary space.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n, k;
    cin >> n >> k;
    --k;
    vector<int> a(n);
    for (int &x : a)
        cin >> x;
    mt19937 random(random_device{}());
    int lo = 0, hi = n;
    while (lo < hi) {
        int pivot = a[uniform_int_distribution<int>(lo, hi - 1)(random)];
        int less = lo, i = lo, greater = hi;
        while (i < greater) {
            if (a[i] < pivot)
                swap(a[less++], a[i++]);
            else if (a[i] > pivot)
                swap(a[i], a[--greater]);
            else
                ++i;
        }
        if (k < less)
            hi = less;
        else if (k >= greater)
            lo = greater;
        else {
            cout << pivot << '\n';
            return 0;
        }
    }
}
~~~
